list_one.evidence:
Lines 83-95:

    final long contentLength = unsignedExtend(in.readInt());   // line 86 — four bytes, the peer's choice
    if(contentLength < Integer.MAX_VALUE) {                     // line 87 — a cast guard, not a limit
        final byte [] buffer = new byte[(int)contentLength];    // line 88 — up to ~2 GiB committed here
        in.readFully(buffer);                                   // line 89 — ... and only now does it fail
    } else {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException(...);            // lines 92-93
    }

The only test applied to contentLength is `< Integer.MAX_VALUE` at line 87. That test exists to stop the cast at line 88 from wrapping negative; it lets every value up to 2147483646 through. Nothing compares the length against a maximum the peer does not set, against the negotiated frame size, or against the bytes actually remaining. The allocation at line 88 precedes the read at line 89, so five bytes on the wire (a type octet plus the four length bytes) commit the array. Reachable through two public entry points and two field-value cases: readLongstr() at lines 108-112 -> readLongstr(in) at 100-104 -> readBytes; readFieldValue case 'S' at line 163 and case 'x' at line 211. `unsignedExtend` (lines 45-49) deliberately clears the sign bit, so the full unsigned 32-bit range reaches the guard.

list_two.evidence:
readTable(), lines 149-152:
    String name = readShortstr(tableIn);
    Object value = readFieldValue(tableIn);
    if(!table.containsKey(name))
        table.put(name, value);
A table that encodes the same name twice is accepted; the second value is discarded without an exception, a log line or any signal to the caller. The choice of first-wins is not stated anywhere in the file, and the wire format has no rule this code is enforcing.

readTable(), lines 142-147:
    long tableLength = unsignedExtend(in.readInt());
    if (tableLength == 0) return Collections.emptyMap();
    DataInputStream tableIn = new DataInputStream(new TruncatedInputStream(in, tableLength));
readArray(), lines 240-242:
    long length = unsignedExtend(in.readInt());
    DataInputStream arrayIn = new DataInputStream(new TruncatedInputStream(in, length));
Both accept 0..4294967295 from the wire and pass it straight through as a truncation limit. Neither compares it with the bytes actually available, and readArray does not even carry the `< Integer.MAX_VALUE` cast guard that readBytes has at line 87 — the same manifest of a length the peer chooses, with three different amounts of checking in one file.

readTimestamp(), line 269:
    return new Date(in.readLong()*1000);
readLong() returns the peer's full signed 64-bit value; the multiplication is done in long arithmetic, which wraps silently in Java. Any value above 9223372036854775 (or below its negative) wraps, so a far-future AMQP timestamp decodes as a Date in the past and the reverse. There is no range check before or after, and no exception is possible.

readBytes(), lines 86-94:
    final long contentLength = unsignedExtend(in.readInt());
    if(contentLength < Integer.MAX_VALUE) {
        final byte [] buffer = new byte[(int)contentLength];
        in.readFully(buffer);
        return buffer;
    } else {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Very long byte vectors and strings not currently supported");
    }
The only guard on contentLength is `< Integer.MAX_VALUE`, which stops the cast from wrapping and lets every value below ~2 GiB through. The allocation happens before readFully() has read a single content byte, so the bytes actually available are never consulted. Reached from readLongstr() (public API, line 108), from field values of type 'S' (line 163) and 'x' (line 211), and from readTable()/readArray() through readFieldValue().
